<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

    <worksheet margin="1in" bottom="1.25cm" xml:id="linear-systems-complete">
    <title>Linear Systems (Complete)</title>
        <page>
        
            <exercise workspace="2in">
            <title>Motivation</title>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                
                    </p>
                </statement>
                <solution>
                    <p>
                        Systems of linear equations are common in application. Matrices provide a standard way for representing 
                        these systems, as well as efficiently solving them.
                    </p>
                </solution>
            </exercise>

            <exercise workspace="6in">
            <title>Example: Balancing A Chemical Reaction</title>
                <statement>
                    <p>
               <md>
                x_1 CH_4 + x_2O_2 \to x_3CO_2 + x_4H_2O
               </md>
               
                
                    </p>
                </statement>
                <solution>
                    <p>
                        Balancing this reaction reduces to solving the linear system of equations
                        <md>
                            <mrow>4x_1 - 2x_4 \amp = 0 \text{ (balancing Hydrogen)} </mrow>
                            <mrow>x_1 - x_3 \amp = 0 \text{ (balancing Carbon)} </mrow>
                            <mrow>2x_2 - 2x_3 - x_4 \amp = 0 \text{ (balancing Oxygen)} </mrow>
                        </md>
                        We can encode this system in an <term>augmented matrix</term>, 
                        <md>
                         \left[ \begin{array}{rrrr|r}4 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp -2 \amp 0 \\ 1 \amp 0 \amp -1  \amp 0 \amp 0 \\ 0 \amp 2 \amp -2  \amp -1 \amp 0 \\\end{array} \right].
                        </md>
                    </p>
                    <p>
                        We can also <url href="https://www.desmos.com/3d/6ia6xz9hrb">view this system geometrically</url>, which can help us intuit solution behavior.
                    </p>
                </solution>
            </exercise>
</page>
<page>
            <exercise workspace="3in">
                <title>Example: Augmented Matrix as a Linear System</title>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        Write the matrix 

                        <md>
                         \left[ \begin{array}{rrr|r}1 \amp 2 \amp 4 \amp 2 \\ 1 \amp 0 \amp -2  \amp -1 \\ -2 \amp 2 \amp 8  \amp 2 \\\end{array} \right].
                        </md>
                        as a linear system of equations.
                    </p>
                </statement> 
            <solution>
                <p>
                    This represents the system
                    <md>
                            <mrow>x_1 +2x_2 + 4x_3 \amp = 2  </mrow>
                            <mrow>x_1 - 2x_3 \amp = -1  </mrow>
                            <mrow>-2x_1 + 2x_2 +8x_3 \amp = 2  </mrow>
                        </md>
                </p>
            </solution>
          
            </exercise>
</page>
<page>
        <exercise workspace="3in">
        <title>Solutions to Linear Systems</title>
        <statement>
            <p>
                A <term>solution</term> to a linear system <m>A\xvec = \bvec</m> is 
            </p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
            <p>
                a vector <m>\cvec</m> such that <m>A\cvec = \bvec</m>. Geometrically, solutions are intersection points. 
                We'll focus on the details of finding solutions in the next section, but general behavior should we expect for 
                solutions linear systems?
            </p>
        </solution>
            
        </exercise>

         <exercise>
        <title>Thought Exercise: Solutions to Linear Systems</title>
        <task workspace="2in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    In 2D, picture two lines. How many intersection points are possible? If you picture 3 lines, does your
                    answer change?
                </p>
            </statement>
            <solution>
                <p>
                    <m>0,1,\infty</m>. It is the same for three lines.
                </p>
            </solution>
        </task>

         <task workspace="2in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    In 3D, picture two planes. How many intersection points are possible? If you picture 3 planes, does your
                    answer change? 4 planes?
                </p>
            </statement>
            <solution>
                <p>
                    <m>0,\infty</m>. With 3 or more planes, <m>0,1,\infty</m>.
                </p>
            </solution>
        </task>
        
            
        </exercise>
</page>
<page>

        <exercise workspace="5in">
        <title>Summary: Solutions to Linear Systems</title>
       <statement>
        <p>
            
        </p>
       </statement>
       <solution>
        <p>
            <ol>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        The solution space to a linear system has only three possibilities:
                        <ol>
                            <li>
                                <p>
                                    No solutions. This is called an <term>inconsistent system</term>.
                                </p>
                            </li>
                            <li>
                                <p>
                                    Exactly one solution.
                                </p>
                            </li>
                            <li>
                                <p>
                                    Infinitely many solutions.
                                </p>
                            </li>
                        </ol>
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        The general trends are that if there are many more rows than columns (a <q>tall</q> matrix), it is likely
                        the system is inconsistent. If there are many more columns than rows (a <q>wide</q> matrix), it is likely
                        there are many solutions. 
                    </p>
                    <p>
                        These are just trends, and there are exceptions to both of these rules of thumb, as we've already reasoned 
                        in this section.
                    </p>
                </li>
            </ol>
        </p>
       </solution>
    </exercise>
    
    </page>

   

       
    </worksheet>
